    "math_academy_id": "q-83728",
    "knowledge_point_id": "fce4cd5a-537a-5eb4-a610-59d2bad95a89",
    "knowledge_point": "Deducing the Second-Degree Taylor Polynomial Given the Graph of a Function",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037233/assets/bb52a7975c917c7fe310eb29ae1daaa3438a906b84771ae5ce6998c055c4e6f7.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following could be the second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f$ about $x=3?$",
    "worked_solution": "The second-degree Taylor polynomial for $f(x)$ about $x=3$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(3)+{f}^{′}(3)(x-3)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(3)}{2}(x-3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we observe:\n\n- $f(3)>0.$ Therefore, the first term of the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{′}(3)>0,$ because the slope of the tangent at $x=3$ is positive. Therefore, the coefficient of the second term in the Taylor polynomial must be positive.\n\n- ${f}^{″}(3)<0,$ because the graph is concave down at $x=3.$ Therefore, the coefficient of the third term in the Taylor polynomial must be negative.\n\nThe only option that meets the above criteria is\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=3+2(x-3)-(x-3)^{2}.\n$$",
